rences can still create friction. Mature systems therefore pair standards with conformance testing, capability negotiation, and careful version management.\n\n## Engineering consequences\n\nA practical design should begin with explicit requirements and observable success criteria. Capacity, latency, correctness, recovery time, security boundaries, cost, and maintainability often pull in different directions. Teams should identify which of those dimensions are critical for the workload, then test the system under conditions that challenge those assumptions. That includes degraded dependencies, bursty demand, incomplete data, invalid inputs, and operational mistakes. The exercise matters because architecture that looks elegant under normal conditions can behave very differently when queues grow, caches miss, credentials expire, replicas lag, or networks partition.\n\nAnother useful distinction is between a mechanism and an outcome. A mechanism such as replication, encryption, caching, parallelism, isolation, or validation can contribute to a desired outcome, but it does not guarantee that outcome by itself. Reliability depends on failure independence and recovery. Security depends on policy and lifecycle controls as well as cryptography. Performance depends on end-to-end bottlenecks rather than the speed of one component. Interoperability depends on behavior, not merely the presence of a standard name.\n\n## What to remember\n\nPasskey authentication should be evaluated in the context of the larger system around it. The strongest implementations make assumptions explicit, expose useful telemetry, constrain failure, and leave room for change. For how standards shape interoperability, the central lesson is to measure the behavior that matters and to design around real constraints instead of relying on labels or idealized diagrams.\n\nThis article is an evergreen technical explainer. Product capabilities and implementation details can change, so standards and primary technical documentation should be checked when making production decisions.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/tech-fallback-2.png","featured_image_alt":"Passkey authentication: How standards shape interoperability","word_count":508,"reading_time_minutes":2,"keywords":["technology","evergreen","explainer","Passkey authentication"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"url":"https://www.nist.gov/","title":"National Institute of Standards and Technology","position":0,"provider":"NIST","authority":"primary"},{"url":"https://www.w3.org/","title":"World Wide Web Consortium","position":1,"provider":"W3C","authority":"primary"},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/digital/","title":"OECD Digital Economy","position":2,"provider":"OECD","authority":"primary"}]},{"external_id":"tech-archive-2026-147","published_at":"2026-06-22 12:00:00+00","slug":"tech-archive-2026-147","title":"Passkey authentication: What trade offs operators must manage","description":"Passkey authentication explained through the lens of what trade offs operators must manage, with emphasis on mechanisms, engineering trade-offs, reliability, security, and practical operation.","body_markdown":"# Passkey authentication: What trade offs operators must manage\n\nPasskey authentication is easier to reason about when it is treated as a system of constraints rather than a product label. The useful questions are what information or resources move through it, which boundaries matter, what assumptions are being made, and how failure changes the expected behavior. This guide focuses on what trade offs operators must manage while keeping the wider engineering context visible.\n\n## Core concept\n\nPasskeys are credentials based on public-key cryptography that let users authenticate without sending a reusable password to a server. A service stores a public key while the private credential remains protected by an authenticator and is used to sign a challenge for the correct relying party. This design resists many phishing and credential-reuse attacks because a passkey is bound to the service identity. Deployment still requires careful work around device enrollment, synchronization, recovery, account linking, legacy login paths, and user education. The security of a passkey system is only as strong as its weakest recovery route.\n\n## What trade offs operators must manage\n\nOperating passkey authentication means balancing goals that cannot all be maximized at once. More redundancy can increase resilience but also cost and complexity. Stronger consistency can simplify reasoning but add latency or reduce availability during partitions. Aggressive caching can improve speed while increasing staleness risk. Rich telemetry helps diagnosis but consumes storage and can expose sensitive information. The operational task is to choose explicit priorities, measure whether the system meets them, and revisit those priorities as scale and risk change. Hidden trade-offs are usually more dangerous than acknowledged ones.\n\n## Engineering consequences\n\nA practical design should begin with explicit requirements and observable success criteria. Capacity, latency, correctness, recovery time, security boundaries, cost, and maintainability often pull in different directions. Teams should identify which of those dimensions are critical for the workload, then test the system under conditions that challenge those assumptions. That includes degraded dependencies, bursty demand, incomplete data, invalid inputs, and operational mistakes. The exercise matters because architecture that looks elegant under normal conditions can behave very differently when queues grow, caches miss, credentials expire, replicas lag, or networks partition.\n\nAnother useful distinction is between a mechanism and an outcome. A mechanism such as replication, encryption, caching, parallelism, isolation, or validation can contribute to a desired outcome, but it does not guarantee that outcome by itself. Reliability depends on failure independence and recovery. Security depends on policy and lifecycle controls as well as cryptography. Performance depends on end-to-end bottlenecks rather than the speed of one component. Interoperability depends on behavior, not merely the presence of a standard name.\n\n## What to remember\n\nPasskey authentication should be evaluated in the context of the larger system around it. The strongest implementations make assumptions explicit, expose useful telemetry, constrain failure, and leave room for change. For what trade offs operators must manage, the central lesson is to measure the behavior that matters and to design around real constraints instead of relying on labels or idealized diagrams.\n\nThis article is an evergreen technical explainer. Product capabilities and implementation details can change, so standards and primary technical documentation should be checked when making production decisions.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/tech-fallback-3.png","featured_image_alt":"Passkey authentication: What trade offs operators must manage","word_count":530,"reading_time_minutes":2,"keywords":["technology","evergreen","explainer","Passkey authentication"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"url":"https://www.nist.gov/","title":"National Institute of Standards and Technology","position":0,"provider":"NIST","authority":"primary"},{"url":"https://www.w3.org/","title":"World Wide Web Consortium","position":1,"provider":"W3C","authority":"primary"},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/digital/","title":"OECD Digital Economy","position":2,"provider":"OECD","authority":"primary"}]},{"external_id":"tech-archive-2026-148","published_at":"2026-06-23 12:00:00+00","slug":"tech-archive-2026-148","title":"Privacy preserving computation: How it works","description":"Privacy preserving computation explained through the lens of how it works, with emphasis on mechanisms, engineering trade-offs, reliability, security, and practical operation.","body_markdown":"# Privacy preserving computation: How it works\n\nPrivacy preserving computation is easier to reason about when it is treated as a system of constraints rather than a product label. The useful questions are what information or resources move through it, which boundaries matter, what assumptions are being made, and how failure changes the expected behavior. This guide focuses on how it works while keeping the wider engineering context visible.\n\n## Core concept\n\nPrivacy preserving computation is a broad family of techniques intended to extract useful results while reducing exposure of sensitive data. Approaches include secure multiparty computation, homomorphic encryption, trusted execution environments, differential privacy, and carefully designed aggregation. Each protects against a different threat model and carries different computational or accuracy costs. The phrase should not be treated as a single guarantee. A sound design begins by defining which parties are trusted, what an attacker can observe, what outputs are released, and which forms of leakage are acceptable.\n\n## How it works\n\nTo understand how privacy preserving computation works, separate the visible service from the mechanisms underneath it. Inputs enter through defined interfaces, internal components transform or route those inputs, state is maintained where necessary, and outputs are checked against expected constraints. Engineers care about the path of data and control because each boundary can add latency, ambiguity, or failure. The most useful mental model is therefore a chain of responsibilities rather than a single black box. Once those responsibilities are visible, it becomes easier to see which component owns correctness, where observability belongs, and how the system should behave when one dependency is degraded.\n\n## Engineering consequences\n\nA practical design should begin with explicit requirements and observable success criteria. Capacity, latency, correctness, recovery time, security boundaries, cost, and maintainability often pull in different directions. Teams should identify which of those dimensions are critical for the workload, then test the system under conditions that challenge those assumptions. That includes degraded dependencies, bursty demand, incomplete data, invalid inputs, and operational mistakes. The exercise matters because architecture that looks elegant under normal conditions can behave very differently when queues grow, caches miss, credentials expire, replicas lag, or networks partition.\n\nAnother useful distinction is between a mechanism and an outcome. A mechanism such as replication, encryption, caching, parallelism, isolation, or validation can contribute to a desired outcome, but it does not guarantee that outcome by itself. Reliability depends on failure independence and recovery. Security depends on policy and lifecycle controls as well as cryptography. Performance depends on end-to-end bottlenecks rather than the speed of one component. Interoperability depends on behavior, not merely the presence of a standard name.\n\n## What to remember\n\nPrivacy preserving computation should be evaluated in the context of the larger system around it. The strongest implementations make assumptions explicit, expose useful telemetry, constrain failure, and leave room for change. For how it works, the central lesson is to measure the behavior that matters and to design around real constraints instead of relying on labels or idealized diagrams.\n\nThis article is an evergreen technical explainer. Product capabilities and implementation details can change, so standards and primary technical documentation should be checked when making production decisions.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/tech-fallback-1.png","featured_image_alt":"Privacy preserving computation: How it works","word_count":525,"reading_time_minutes":2,"keywords":["technology","evergreen","explainer","Privacy preserving computation"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"url":"https://www.nist.gov/","title":"National Institute of Standards and Technology","position":0,"provider":"NIST","authority":"primary"},{"url":"https://www.w3.org/","title":"World Wide Web Consortium","position":1,"provider":"W3C","authority":"primary"},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/digital/","title":"OECD Digital Economy","position":2,"provider":"OECD","authority":"primary"}]},{"external_id":"tech-archive-2026-149","published_at":"2026-06-24 12:00:00+00","slug":"tech-archive-2026-149","title":"Privacy preserving computation: Why architecture matters","description":"Privacy preserving computation explained through the lens of why architecture matters, with emphasis on mechanisms, engineering trade-offs, reliability, security, and practical operation.","body_markdown":"# Privacy preserving computation: Why architecture matters\n\nPrivacy preserving computation is easier to reason about when it is treated as a system of constraints rather than a product label. The useful questions are what information or resources move through it, which boundaries matter, what assumptions are being made, and how failure changes the expected behavior. This guide focuses on why architecture matters while keeping the wider engineering context visible.\n\n## Core concept\n\nPrivacy preserving computation is a broad family of techniques intended to extract useful results while reducing exposure of sensitive data. Approaches include secure multiparty computation, homomorphic encryption, trusted execution environments, differential privacy, and carefully designed aggregation. Each protects against a different threat model and carries different computational or accuracy costs. The phrase should not be treated as a single guarantee. A sound design begins by defining which parties are trusted, what an attacker can observe, what outputs are released, and which forms of leakage are acceptable.\n\n## Why architecture matters\n\nArchitecture matters for privacy preserving computation because early boundary decisions determine what can later be changed independently. A design that centralizes every responsibility may be easy to understand at first but can create bottlenecks and broad failure domains. A highly distributed design can scale or isolate faults, yet it increases coordination, deployment, and observability costs. The right structure depends on workload, trust boundaries, expected growth, and operational maturity. Good architecture makes important trade-offs explicit, keeps interfaces narrow, and gives teams a realistic way to test failure instead of assuming the happy path will dominate production.\n\n## Engineering consequences\n\nA practical design should begin with explicit requirements and observable success criteria. Capacity, latency, correctness, recovery time, security boundaries, cost, and maintainability often pull in different directions. Teams should identify which of those dimensions are critical for the workload, then test the system under conditions that challenge those assumptions. That includes degraded dependencies, bursty demand, incomplete data, invalid inputs, and operational mistakes. The exercise matters because architecture that looks elegant under normal conditions can behave very differently when queues grow, caches miss, credentials expire, replicas lag, or networks partition.\n\nAnother useful distinction is between a mechanism and an outcome. A mechanism such as replication, encryption, caching, parallelism, isolation, or validation can contribute to a desired outcome, but it does not guarantee that outcome by itself. Reliability depends on failure independence and recovery. Security depends on policy and lifecycle controls as well as cryptography. Performance depends on end-to-end bottlenecks rather than the speed of one component. Interoperability depends on behavior, not merely the presence of a standard name.\n\n## What to remember\n\nPrivacy preserving computation should be evaluated in the context of the larger system around it. The strongest implementations make assumptions explicit, expose useful telemetry, constrain failure, and leave room for change. For why architecture matters, the central lesson is to measure the behavior that matters and to design around real constraints instead of relying on labels or idealized diagrams.\n\nThis article is an evergreen technical explainer. Product capabilities and implementation details can change, so standards and primary technical documentation should be checked when making production decisions.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/tech-fallback-2.png","featured_image_alt":"Privacy preserving computation: Why architecture matters","word_count":517,"reading_time_minutes":2,"keywords":["technology","evergreen","explainer","Privacy preserving computation"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"url":"https://www.nist.gov/","title":"National Institute of Standards and Technology","position":0,"provider":"NIST","authority":"primary"},{"url":"https://www.w3.org/","title":"World Wide Web Consortium","position":1,"provider":"W3C","authority":"primary"},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/digital/","title":"OECD Digital Economy","position":2,"provider":"OECD","authority":"primary"}]},{"external_id":"tech-archive-2026-150","published_at":"2026-06-25 12:00:00+00","slug":"tech-archive-2026-150","title":"Privacy preserving computation: Where performance bottlenecks emerge","description":"Privacy preserving computation explained through the lens of where performance bottlenecks emerge, with emphasis on mechanisms, engineering trade-offs, reliability, security, and practical operation.","body_markdown":"# Privacy preserving computation: Where performance bottlenecks emerge\n\nPrivacy preserving computation is easier to reason about when it is treated as a system of constraints rather than a product label. The useful questions are what information or resources move through it, which boundaries matter, what assumptions are being made, and how failure changes the expected behavior. This guide focuses on where performance bottlenecks emerge while keeping the wider engineering context visible.\n\n## Core concept\n\nPrivacy preserving computation is a broad family of techniques intended to extract useful results while reducing exposure of sensitive data. Approaches include secure multiparty computation, homomorphic encryption, trusted execution environments, differential privacy, and carefully designed aggregation. Each protects against a different threat model and carries different computational or accuracy costs. The phrase should not be treated as a single guarantee. A sound design begins by defining which parties are trusted, what an attacker can observe, what outputs are released, and which forms of leakage are acceptable.\n\n## Where performance bottlenecks emerge\n\nPerformance bottlenecks in privacy preserving computation often appear where a finite resource is shared: memory bandwidth, storage I/O, network capacity, lock contention, serialization, queues, or an external dependency. Average latency can hide a system that performs badly under bursts, so tail latency and saturation are often more informative than a single mean. Optimization should start with measurement. Profiling and tracing can distinguish computation from waiting, while load tests reveal where backpressure begins. The goal is not maximum speed at every layer. It is predictable service under the workload the system is actually expected to carry.\n\n## Engineering consequences\n\nA practical design should begin with explicit requirements and observable success criteria. Capacity, latency, correctness, recovery time, security boundaries, cost, and maintainability often pull in different directions. Teams should identify which of those dimensions are critical for the workload, then test the system under conditions that challenge those assumptions. That includes degraded dependencies, bursty demand, incomplete data, invalid inputs, and operational mistakes. The exercise matters because architecture that looks elegant under normal conditions can behave very differently when queues grow, caches miss, credentials expire, replicas lag, or networks partition.\n\nAnother useful distinction is between a mechanism and an outcome. A mechanism such as replication, encryption, caching, parallelism, isolation, or validation can contribute to a desired outcome, but it does not guarantee that outcome by itself. Reliability depends on failure independence and recovery. Security depends on policy and lifecycle controls as well as cryptography. Performance depends on end-to-end bottlenecks rather than the speed of one component. Interoperability depends on behavior, not merely the presence of a standard name.\n\n## What to remember\n\nPrivacy preserving computation should be evaluated in the context of the larger system around it. The strongest implementations make assumptions explicit, expose useful telemetry, constrain failure, and leave room for change. For where performance bottlenecks emerge, the central lesson is to measure the behavior that matters and to design around real constraints instead of relying on labels or idealized diagrams.\n\nThis article is an evergreen technical explainer. Product capabilities and implementation details can change, so standards and primary technical documentation should be checked when making production decisions.","article_type":"explainer","language":"en","featured_image_url":"/fallback-images/tech-fallback-3.png","featured_image_alt":"Privacy preserving computation: Where performance bottlenecks emerge","word_count":521,"reading_time_minutes":2,"keywords":["technology","evergreen","explainer","Privacy preserving computation"],"provider":"chatgpt","model":"gpt-5.6-sol","image_source_type":"editorial-fallback","image_provider":"blogdel","image_model":null,"refs":[{"url":"https://www.nist.gov/","title":"National Institute of Standards and Technology","position":0,"provider":"NIST","authority":"primary"},{"url":"https://www.w3.org/","title":"World Wide Web Consortium","position":1,"provider":"W3C","authority":"primary"},{"url":"https://www.oecd.org/digital/","title":"OECD Digital Economy","position":2,"provider":"OECD","authority":"primary"}]}]$blogdel$::jsonb) as p(
  external_id text,
  published_at timestamptz,
  slug text,
  title text,
  description text,
  body_markdown text,
  article_type text,
  language text,
  featured_image_url text,
  featured_image_alt text,
  word_count integer,
  reading_time_minutes integer,
  keywords text[],
  provider text,
  model text,
  image_source_type text,
  image_provider text,
  image_model text,
  refs jsonb
);

update public.source_items si
set status = 'processed',
    rejection_reason = null,
    instructions = coalesce(si.instructions, '{}'::jsonb) || jsonb_build_object(
      'required_author_provider','chatgpt',
      'required_author_model','gpt-5.6-sol',
      'benchmark_cohort','sol-historical-archive-v1',
      'forbid_blogdel_text_generation',true
    )
from _blogdel_tech_payload p
where si.external_id = p.external_id
  and si.category_id = (select id from public.categories where slug='technology');

update public.articles a
set slug = p.slug,
    title = p.title,
    description = p.description,
    body_markdown = p.body_markdown,
    article_type = p.article_type::public.article_type_enum,
    language = p.language,
    status = 'published'::public.article_status_enum,
    featured_image_url = p.featured_image_url,
    featured_image_alt = p.featured_image_alt,
    word_count = p.word_count,
    reading_time_minutes = p.reading_time_minutes,
    keywords = p.keywords,
    provider = 'chatgpt',
    model = 'gpt-5.6-sol',
    is_demo = false,
    event_at = p.published_at,
    published_at = p.published_at,
    archived_at = null,
    generation_job_id = null,
    image_source_type = p.image_source_type,
    image_provider = p.image_provider,
    image_model = p.image_model,
    updated_at = now()
from _blogdel_tech_payload p
join public.source_items si on si.external_id = p.external_id
where a.source_item_id = si.id;

insert into public.articles (
  source_item_id, category_id, author_id, generation_job_id,
  slug, title, description, body_markdown, article_type, language,
  status, featured_image_url, featured_image_alt, word_count,
  reading_time_minutes, keywords, provider, model, is_demo,
  event_at, published_at, image_source_type, image_provider, image_model
)
select
  si.id,
  si.category_id,
  au.id,
  null,
  p.slug,
  p.title,
  p.description,
  p.body_markdown,
  p.article_type::public.article_type_enum,
  p.language,
  'published'::public.article_status_enum,
  p.featured_image_url,
  p.featured_image_alt,
  p.word_count,
  p.reading_time_minutes,
  p.keywords,
  'chatgpt',
  'gpt-5.6-sol',
  false,
  p.published_at,
  p.published_at,
  p.image_source_type,
  p.image_provider,
  p.image_model
from _blogdel_tech_payload p
join public.source_items si on si.external_id = p.external_id
join public.authors au on au.category_id=si.category_id and au.is_active
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.articles a where a.source_item_id=si.id
);

delete from public.article_references ar
using public.articles a, public.source_items si, _blogdel_tech_payload p
where ar.article_id=a.id
  and a.source_item_id=si.id
  and si.external_id=p.external_id;

insert into public.article_references (
  article_id, source_item_id, provider, title, url, authority, position, retrieved_at
)
select
  a.id,
  si.id,
  ref->>'provider',
  ref->>'title',
  ref->>'url',
  nullif(ref->>'authority',''),
  coalesce((ref->>'position')::integer, ordinality::integer - 1),
  now()
from _blogdel_tech_payload p
join public.source_items si on si.external_id=p.external_id
join public.articles a on a.source_item_id=si.id
cross join lateral jsonb_array_elements(p.refs) with ordinality as r(ref, ordinality);

commit;
